[{"id":"VRCE-7-7-1","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":1,"english":"As clouds assail a mountain with showers, then the Raksasas, representing clouds, setting up shouts, began to assail Nārāyaṇa representing a mountain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.522,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007001","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"058c81f62d10fea8414d4064b2897f12ed92783988718457b9451a9c2f756a10","compact":"e53bc2e836316205c5410b55b50f7d60766d76b7e7b89bf23ed8c0ed976b8f12","nasal":"fbbc4db3ae13386734bc9441ca912cd307c1670c662a078f3e7130e3168b0422"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-2","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":2,"english":"Visnu of a bright brown-blue hue, being surrounded by the flower of the night-rangers with dark visages, resembled the Añjana mountain, on which the clouds pour down.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.845,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007002","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"89a99aa911722730dc12e75f120a27813bdcd69d5a7a79aae7abc22e64cc5c33","compact":"5a8f611b4e7319f86860d228c556273f492128a4acb48ede4b856cf83829d48d","nasal":"55843076613d4f5dfe6b324547a7fefc49d71917975443fc1d624d191ae97beb"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-3","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":3,"english":"As locusts (flood) a field, knots (throng) about a flame, gad-flies (cluster) around a pot of honey, or makaras (range) in the sea, so the shafts shot by the bows of the Rākṣasa, having (the energy of) the thunder-bolt and endowed with (the velocity of) the wind or thought, pierce Hari as if at the universal dissolution.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.533,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007003","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"699dde71c9505eeb0298ca20f86e8b278c8dc895ab4f9bfdb14dac9204eab9d0","compact":"d386ba5d17ca4da635badbd5bf2cc17c9a8bb62889aa9295f4f9574fdeee6313","nasal":"d386ba5d17ca4da635badbd5bf2cc17c9a8bb62889aa9295f4f9574fdeee6313"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-4","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":4,"english":"As locusts (flood) a field, knots (throng) about a flame, gad-flies (cluster) around a pot of honey, or makaras (range) in the sea, so the shafts shot by the bows of the Rākṣasa, having (the energy of) the thunder-bolt and endowed with (the velocity of) the wind or thought, pierce Hari as if at the universal dissolution.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.8,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007004","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3421368dc4b95f6a09441e03eafd4efcf414efb53c6bd6ecfaaaa4b02dc82818","compact":"9631277c958eb5997d27cdc6826712815336515634f3f29db6ca12da775e5ebb","nasal":"155499d63edb28ee15d61b8aeceb1430fedc59ecac21689932a149595fcd7b85"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-5","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":5,"english":"Those mounted on cars (assailed him) with their cars, and those mounted on the backs of elephants (assailed him) with their elephants, and those riding horses (assailed him) with their horses, and the foot-soldiers attacked him, stationed in the sky; and the foremost of Rākşasas resembling mountains, by means of darts, rstis and tomaras, suspended the respiration of Hari even as the prāņāyāma* does that of a twice-born one. *The practice of regulation respiration and finally suspending the same. This is practised by the Yogis of India, who attribute wonderful virtues to it.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.591,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-4"],