ksasa aimlessly strayed hither and thither.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.889,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007026","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0d755587f4eaa28b5404b128440fdda75d0cbcec2c671bf514cae682c458c02b","compact":"63984f1deae862edb4d16211284d6164deb63a2bb01c335869c91165985faf58","nasal":"4ed491c7b037735ded7930fd2a161455a1ff6491d01dcbb0911e8f8bda005735"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-27","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":27,"english":"And with his bewildered steeds Sumali wanders like a person deprived of patience, whose senses are under hallucination.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.613,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007027","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5f3d2ff29a9a44cb317da388a61bbbdbe381617009c85739320d77ce6e2f27fb","compact":"7e3a18ba46f19b096778c68dc3df69aeaeb69b6b890b8f63af16110c54b40e61","nasal":"ca0eba3952f5fdff8c4c5ae8a7a601f4697392ef27dc4178ab9a2e4f3d45177d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-28","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":28,"english":"Like birds entering the Krauñca mountain, the arrows discharged from Mali's bow, graced with gold, hit Hari and entered (into his body).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.505,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-27"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007028","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ea867e6bcf439f66fbe9f36ece1f8e32cc63abcb94a015e60a6e9255e7eef29f","compact":"06861d96260cc900cfda8c792f5581ae48a872e89d1b41ef801bdff43d9bb9ee","nasal":"a83a7f5360ae105fb2a426de532f61c993682dfc8654d1bf508336a71e0de251"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-29","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":29,"english":"Thereat, as a person holding his senses under subjection is not thrown out of his mental balance, Vişnu, assailed in that encounter with thousands of arrows shot by Māli, was not disturbed ever so little.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.91,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-28"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007029","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7cd64fee494153c9b362df4aa6f02a94d78c47acf337969acac0cf2c25383838","compact":"37ae980e18ceebef5fb50603dd2a87b7b2de59ba8a16e8053280d4bf61af1241","nasal":"b3381d14eaa0c212688fd920548946027644abfa89116de40684166b40eedab1"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-30","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":30,"english":"Then that creator of all beings, the reverend Gadādhara,* twanging his bow-string, showered volleys of shafts on Máli. *Lit. the holder of the mace, a name of Visnu.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-29"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007030","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1079082963c4b1fe89da7b84410920cfae6d270dec766062728c066c64a794f7","compact":"05f9cc0cd1e2e42618bd2f12d454dad0e5fb46300fdf374e6ddb5d4cad21a225","nasal":"05f9cc0cd1e2e42618bd2f12d454dad0e5fb46300fdf374e6ddb5d4cad21a225"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-31","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":31,"english":"And getting at Mali's body, the arrows furnished with the luminousness of lightening drink his blood, like to serpents drinking nectar.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-30"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007031","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9a3f43c46caaedaaa8ac2cc6b4e81f22de48626e4eb83b71a96961a5f14f25ce","compact":"ae7b4c6c0c1aa9b68b5eaae9ffdda6b1e7d07eeadd486a739a47a0cfcf3cddf8","nasal":"a1b9e48e060ba881240fc7bd3849d64852694ef6f9bc39f688559b85912a036b"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-32","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":32,"english":"And baffling Māli, the holder of the conch, discus, and mace, brought down Māli's crown, standard, bow and steeds.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.393,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-31"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007032","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"05e589176854b87fd9a55dfda6704e18df9d51d637cf180210557dd6535c0f4c","compact":"5a0af4e16647b8861933a00417bbbb77144cad6f89f93ecd44da2c907dc78122","nasal":"5a0af4e16647b8861933a00417bbbb77144cad6f89f93ecd44da2c907dc78122"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-33","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":33,"english":"Then that foremost of night-rangers, on being deprived of his car, sprang forward, mace in hand, even as lion bounds up from the brow of a hillock.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.806,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007033","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc04645a8c426aa5583912c22fe846c401e252266dbcc70e14615d44380ae61e","compact":"76540cf28cde57b443dbd97ae4b298aa78df9328b6b00ea47fa96a80424aba37","nasal":"76540cf28cde57b443dbd97ae4b298aa78df9328b6b00ea47fa96a80424aba37"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-34","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":34,"english":"He with his mace smote at the forehead of that lord of birds, even as the Destroyer had smitten Isana,* or as Indra smites a mountain with his thunder-bolt. *There took place an encounter betwcen Radra and Yama in the Sveta forest, for the protection of Märkandeya and a certain king. In this battle, Yama had smitten Rudra. This is narrated in the Puranas.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.561,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007034","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5394f7c0c3bf4b8efb55be7de7f8e69a7f6d4d45176db2a382fb982def8b3d07","compact":"0efb2a71dcc82c40e4fb8d95f0235b0c2531d6fad995c76148af7f3fb615707e","nasal":"c68d19b21b3116c2e60ba6cd75ef0d678e8abe12b5dab5034380052f9130f43f"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-35","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":35,"english":"On being severely struck by Mali with his mace, Garuda, writhing in torments, moved the god away from the field of battle.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007035","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"352e1367caf1001c1aa691e2322a5720251e5b07e163ee26fe3ff6748f45261d","compact":"0bc9fad0575c3cfa103c9b2dfa63e98fa20bb5839d8868e3400a8b039a00bb23","nasal":"0bc9fad0575c3cfa103c9b2dfa63e98fa20bb5839d8868e3400a8b039a00bb23"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-36","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":36,"english":"On the god having been removed from the field by Mäli as well as Garuda, there arose a mighty din of Raksasa roaring (in chorus).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.842,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-35"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007036","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"77a63630acbe0dcdc7261d6c26b950e21a12414578ee45e4cba0d6086ef82e12","compact":"e0322641fb62e846be5be72678057376c20c1f76e64216f4b474460da0790b70","nasal":"e0322641fb62e846be5be72678057376c20c1f76e64216f4b474460da0790b70"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-37","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":37,"english":"Hearing the cheers of the Raksasa, Indra's younger brother, the reverend Hari, sitting on that lord of birds, albeit moved off from the scene of conflict, waxing enraged, from a desire of slaying (Mali) hurled his disc at him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.422,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-36"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007037","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"19e99564cce28b403dc50049697b3b9ba125b3ddeecb16f4c108f04161230dab","compact":"e739378f9c1af11af5d5189b110affe8bafa185c172df79ae0a493af5b0af908","nasal":"e739378f9c1af11af5d5189b110affe8bafa185c172df79ae0a493af5b0af908"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-38","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":38,"english":"Thereat the disc, possessed of the splendour of the solar disc, and resembling the wheel itself of Kāla, flooding all the heavens with radiance, brought down Māli's head to the ground.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-37"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007038","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5e0ff0c28a61a055eacace5358d94ebc1b58cd138085dc82fe3775409407f303","compact":"fa44eb1a9b3ecb730340bef7ae631b7dbf3c974dae50bf6e76f4b7fca98bb7c3","nasal":"62f3398b15e7ede81be515b09e7a25d0e960ea5760ee90306e3b15a5398077b9"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-39","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":39,"english":"That head of lord of Rākşasas, terrific to behold, on being cut off (by Visnu), vomiting blood, fell down before (the Rākşasas), as formerly Rahu's head had fallen.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-38"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007039","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0cf617ec2be67b2dcc919b6e183639c95a0049b5d66f3278dd2033ba7403f2b7","compact":"f9529a335318a6d740e651d0db5bb7c9074aa48f90d998188ce17a144fe8d86c","nasal":"2bb8844468aead719a685cc8ad9e895e48897764f1a27baf159d66eae93a051d"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-40","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":40,"english":"Then the deities experiencing excess of joy set up leonine cheers with might and main, crying,' Excellent, O god.' Seeing Mali slain, Sumali and Mālyavån, burning in grief, fled precipitately towards Lankä along with their forces.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.836,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-39"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007040","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bc39240b0d5574d33cbff6f95cade2dd7ecc80fe09f6bfbcad23d2d465c0cdcc","compact":"22b7d319feec73d563a407a25bf52d9b558c3ba17ce9ac20fd1eb81fa167ec88","nasal":"22b7d319feec73d563a407a25bf52d9b558c3ba17ce9ac20fd1eb81fa167ec88"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-41","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":41,"english":"Then the deities experiencing excess of joy set up leonine cheers with might and main, crying,' Excellent, O god.' Seeing Mali slain, Sumali and Mālyavån, burning in grief, fled precipitately towards Lankä along with their forces.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.65,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-39"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007041","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cf91bfbfa4a8dd5b852043dfef51185bb4d56025e57ec5086de2f1804c6ec14d","compact":"3fb298c031f2ef45419238c05b8e827ad7e83a542351966ec799acc62c333e0a","nasal":"defc81b99d1bcca5c05375bb728da7175b7557bdb40d40abede9272106f4b154"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-42","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":42,"english":"Garuda, being cheered, turned round, and growing enraged, as before drove the Rākşasas with the wind of his wings.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.77,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-40"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007042","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"17c6e19456f9cc02f9c8d282c0c24d50c66c859b0b50589490f8ec4a55e5f49b","compact":"5cd5926d6c0745db63f2e6cd971afd1693583df051455facad4f230057fabe96","nasal":"92d891470d460cd491577b1e39d2ad05d784c7feb756db9ed202775a637c4248"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-43","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":43,"english":"And as the thunder-bolt with lightening (riveth) a mighty mass of clouds, Narayana by means of the thunder-bolts of his arrows discharged from his bow, rived the night-rangers with their hair dishevelled and streaming (in the wind).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.614,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-42"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007043","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1e768a6e194a149a3a33c2ba3367e5976f2c5f15fca0159b72ddf15c6e3c1d94","compact":"149ec71d258f4448d4664f14fa69b62930835ed13a75ee2c9b410c7f7ff239f7","nasal":"e05ccf55b6b1f4e92c90ca63f88f7b3b66ddf25429fbe2628bf5a9720b3cca7c"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-44","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":44,"english":"The forces having their umbrellas rent, their arms falling off, their goodly garments scattered, their entrails coming out and their eyes rolling, became incapable of distinguishing their own party from that of the foe.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.733,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-43"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007044","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a11af7844c252a1d616cf9e7aab7390d9834636f56f1804b4b3dc5cad53d3af9","compact":"b8ec50e157844ac026f76a4607cc72d1aef3da3e9de9f3303a1eedf9e3916443","nasal":"3c635c3fbac73bc7fc022be279de7dfcb26f62fca72887105df25def6c1c829e"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-45","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":45,"english":"Even like (the roars and the vehemence) displayed by elephants on being attacked by lions, the roars, and the vehemence of the nightrangers and their elephants, sore assailed by the Primaeval Lion,* were equal. *Vişnu as a man-lion had formerly taken the conceit out of Hiranyakasipu the Asura king-and slain him, to rid his devotee Prahlada of his mortal foc in the from of a father.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-44"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007045","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f0ff8e0c24609033890af53a6561748b811e5784d46feacc302f40a87a188fb7","compact":"a6e9ee1f03cc2d33607d362e7611cf22ffe6ff27309c0ffa6af5fd0499fe6adb","nasal":"9311477b0d7a6e3688f5fea67c673cbb3c5a80a231692a9f4d7248f8ac618588"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-46","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":46,"english":"And driven by Hari's networks of arrows, and discharging (all the while) their own showers of shafts, those night-rangers resembling the clouds of doom, are driven like the veritable clouds of the universal dissolution drifted by the wind.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.88,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-45"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007046","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9ce1e98331bd3f944fb7857f964b97afe42ac2311e65debc1591f44ce0f9c22e","compact":"289f1b761008bd4d61b4e03f733e076e8b8e9b6e60aa709d3dcd8e56e6b8378b","nasal":"8e7f7d5039e0c76c79a0eb809898b4c8a9c8983b8193b44f6855441f1173c372"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-47","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":47,"english":"Their heads cut by discus, their parts of bodies crushed with the mace, and hewn in twain with swords, thus they foremost of Rākşasas fell like to hills (toppling headlong).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.822,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-46"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007047","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4d4297810090e5c7740d27c20d9ebd76229db6194f1738df79adb06dbc81c71a","compact":"f2574aabc386595314c3dcf25a815ce623d2f3a2d725b46cdb72e8cf7be6db66","nasal":"ead6026b4f27eef9a006ed3bd45ee8d1bad10e641e4564c604487af6ff639d78"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-48","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":48,"english":"And (some) with their lotus-faces cut by the discus, (some) with their chests crushed with the mace, (some) with their necks torn off by the plough, (some) with their heads riven by the club, and some hewn by the sword, and others pierced by arrows, the Rākṣasas began to drop fast from the sky into the waters of the ocean.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-41"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007048","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"45f7d36e5c1f2d546bfd22e4674cef39f1bbabcc5b1762711a0fa83e9aef94e7","compact":"a420285c379b60aeaeb93e70528d2b27af2426be6a90816203a621aa84084bf1","nasal":"88c80dc6472cb0a8e47615e9b4c0964c906c4784c8bd1247e613215acaac5b2a"}},{"id":"VRCE-7-7-49","kanda":7,"kandaId":"uttara","sarga":7,"verse":49,"english":"And (some) with their lotus-faces cut by the discus, (some) with their chests crushed with the mace, (some) with their necks torn off by the plough, (some) with their heads riven by the club, and some hewn by the sword, and others pierced by arrows, the Rākṣasas began to drop fast from the sky into the waters of the ocean.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.884,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-7-7-41"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"7007049","sanskritFile":"Ram07.txt",